1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,879
Does school hold back the bright kids? Let's jump straight into this fascinating and truly

2
00:00:04,879 --> 00:00:07,000
fundamental question for our society's future.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,839
Today we explore an extremely rigorous economics study, because it analyzes how

4
00:00:11,839 --> 00:00:16,059
teachers' expectations can almost inadvertently influence the path of

5
00:00:16,059 --> 00:00:19,440
students in Italy, effectively altering equality of opportunity.

6
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:23,839
The analysis focuses on the guidance recommendations given at the end of

7
00:00:23,839 --> 00:00:27,920
middle school — a critical transition that indelibly marks

8
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:30,320
long-term educational trajectories.

9
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:33,240
And here is the real mystery the study starts from.

10
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:37,520
Do schools really offer everyone the same opportunities when grades are equal?

11
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:41,280
Like a detective novel, but based on real data, we look for clues

12
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:45,880
to see whether a family's socioeconomic status quietly shifts recommendations

13
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:47,680
toward the most demanding academic high schools.

14
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:51,680
The somewhat unsettling hypothesis is that this happens even when the academic performance

15
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:54,640
of two pupils is absolutely identical.

16
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:58,520
First section: the gap in recommendations.

17
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:03,000
Looking at objective administrative data, the first clue emerges clearly.

18
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,719
Focusing on the top tier — students in the best 20% in mathematics —

19
00:01:07,719 --> 00:01:09,400
an unmistakable figure appears.

20
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:14,000
Only 51% of students from disadvantaged backgrounds receive advice

21
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,240
to enroll in a scientific or classical liceo.

22
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:20,480
By contrast, the share rises to 62% for their peers from

23
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:24,400
advantaged families — same excellent skills, a huge gap.

24
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:27,439
And there is a further detail to notice.

25
00:01:27,439 --> 00:01:31,600
This gap is not static at all; it grows significantly as

26
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:32,600
grades rise.

27
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:36,800
Even more surprising, the penalty is clearly wider

28
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:39,159
for talented boys than for girls.

29
00:01:39,159 --> 00:01:43,800
We face a genuine empirical mystery that needs an explanation.

30
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:48,600
Second section: hypothetical profiles and the experimental test.

31
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,400
To solve this mystery and find the culprit, researchers had to eliminate

32
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,680
so-called confounding variables.

33
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:55,880
They asked themselves:

34
00:01:55,880 --> 00:02:00,720
what if this gap only reflected different family aspirations? The crucial point

35
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:05,040
is the design of an ingenious experiment based on hypothetical profiles — the famous

36
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:06,040
vignettes.

37
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:09,920
Teachers were asked to evaluate the profile of a hypothetical student — let's call him

38
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:10,920
Matteo.

39
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:15,199
His passion for robotics, his ambitions and his excellent grades were

40
00:02:15,199 --> 00:02:18,199
kept perfectly constant in every case.

41
00:02:18,199 --> 00:02:22,199
The only detail randomized was the education level of

42
00:02:22,199 --> 00:02:23,199
his parents.

43
00:02:23,199 --> 00:02:26,119
In one case university graduates, in the other with a middle-school diploma.

44
00:02:26,119 --> 00:02:31,119
And here is the smoking gun — incontrovertible proof.

45
00:02:31,119 --> 00:02:35,319
Simply changing the socioeconomic cue in the profile text causes a drop of

46
00:02:35,319 --> 00:02:40,099
about 6.2 percentage points in the probability that Matteo is recommended for a demanding

47
00:02:40,099 --> 00:02:41,099
liceo.

48
00:02:41,099 --> 00:02:45,120
Because it is an experiment with the exact same student profile, this shows

49
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:49,439
unambiguously a causal reaction tied purely to family status.

50
00:02:49,439 --> 00:02:52,200
But why does this reaction happen?

51
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:55,840
Text analysis of the motivations written by teachers offers a very

52
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:56,840
clear explanation.

53
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:01,560
The texts reveal that teachers tend to infer — wrongly — a lack of parental

54
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:06,040
support, or a presumed need for immediate entry into work, all

55
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:09,480
based only on that small socioeconomic cue.

56
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:14,159
In short, expectations are recalibrated downward even without real information

57
00:03:14,159 --> 00:03:15,159
about the family.

58
00:03:15,159 --> 00:03:19,520
Third section: expectations versus reality.

59
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:24,879
Now we reach the heart of the analysis: checking objectively whether these expectations

60
00:03:24,879 --> 00:03:28,719
actually match reality, or reflect a distorted perception.

61
00:03:28,719 --> 00:03:32,919
The data reveal a massive perception gap.

62
00:03:32,919 --> 00:03:37,120
When asked to estimate first-year failure rates for high-achieving disadvantaged students

63
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:42,719
enrolled in the most selective licei, the estimates exceed the true rate by over 225%.

64
00:03:42,719 --> 00:03:47,520
That is a gigantic excess of pessimism, completely contradicted by the administrative

65
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:48,759
data on real promotions.

66
00:03:48,759 --> 00:03:53,659
Moreover, those who historically show the strongest biases in

67
00:03:53,659 --> 00:03:57,719
recommendations tend to significantly underestimate their own decision gaps.

68
00:03:57,719 --> 00:04:01,479
We face a clear missing-feedback problem.

69
00:04:01,479 --> 00:04:06,439
In practice, decisions are made under deep uncertainty, without ever receiving

70
00:04:06,439 --> 00:04:11,520
systematic information on the later success of pupils advised years earlier.

71
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:14,719
Section 4: the field intervention.

72
00:04:14,719 --> 00:04:19,480
To correct these distortions, researchers ran a large-scale controlled

73
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:21,519
experiment.

74
00:04:21,519 --> 00:04:25,519
The study involved over a thousand teachers, just before they issued

75
00:04:25,519 --> 00:04:27,720
real guidance recommendations.

76
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:29,720
The sample was split into three.

77
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:32,480
A control group received general reports.

78
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:36,959
A second group received a personalized report on their own historical socioeconomic

79
00:04:36,959 --> 00:04:42,000
gaps, and a third group, besides their historical gaps, also received

80
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,079
an objective comparison with real failure rates, precisely to correct

81
00:04:46,079 --> 00:04:47,759
those false beliefs.

82
00:04:47,759 --> 00:04:51,519
Fifth and final section: the experimental results.

83
00:04:51,519 --> 00:04:56,719
Introducing this simple but powerful data-based feedback loop produced

84
00:04:56,719 --> 00:04:58,240
results of huge impact.

85
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:02,339
The full treatment — combining awareness of one's own gaps with

86
00:05:02,339 --> 00:05:06,199
real failure data — nearly wipes out the socioeconomic gap

87
00:05:06,199 --> 00:05:10,759
in recommendations, and the effect is especially strong among those who in the past

88
00:05:10,759 --> 00:05:12,120
showed the largest gaps.

89
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:17,759
The crucial point is that this intervention successfully reallocated recommendations

90
00:05:17,759 --> 00:05:22,159
toward disadvantaged but talented boys — the group initially most penalized.

91
00:05:22,159 --> 00:05:26,319
The intervention concretely raised their enrollment in demanding licei,

92
00:05:26,319 --> 00:05:30,319
and the data clearly show this happened without any short-run decline

93
00:05:30,319 --> 00:05:34,879
in their academic performance — a measurable success.

94
00:05:34,879 --> 00:05:39,319
To close, a deeper reflection remains: how can the smart use of

95
00:05:39,319 --> 00:05:42,040
data unlock the potential of future generations?

96
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:47,199
This research shows beyond doubt that giving objective, personalized feedback

97
00:05:47,199 --> 00:05:51,480
to those who make fundamental decisions is a powerful tool to correct unconscious

98
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:55,519
biases and actively restore equality of opportunity.

99
00:05:55,519 --> 00:06:00,120
It is a simple approach, but essential to ensure talent is not held back,

100
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,879
but encouraged to thrive.

101
00:06:01,879 --> 00:06:03,583
Thanks for following this exploration.

